WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.917
This paper studies two workhorse optimizers,
momentum SGD and AdaGrad.

00:00:05.997 --> 00:00:10.315
Despite practical success, their non-convex theory lags.

00:00:10.395 --> 00:00:15.576
The authors prove both converge almost surely to
stationary points,

00:00:15.656 --> 00:00:18.560
and show why momentum accelerates SGD.

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:25.041
Momentum SGD and AdaGrad consistently beat plain
SGD, yet their theory is incomplete.

00:00:25.121 --> 00:00:30.641
For possibly non-convex losses, existing analyses
prove only weak guarantees,

00:00:30.721 --> 00:00:33.913
like subsequence or time-average convergence,

00:00:33.993 --> 00:00:37.840
not the almost-sure convergence practitioners rely on.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:41.908
Why now?

00:00:41.988 --> 00:00:45.676
Prior momentum proofs need a coefficient shrinking
to zero,

00:00:45.756 --> 00:00:49.444
but practitioners fix it near zero point nine.

00:00:49.524 --> 00:00:53.212
Prior AdaGrad results cover a modified form, not
the standard one.

00:00:53.292 --> 00:00:56.980
It drops convexity, bounded-iterate, and
bounded-noise assumptions.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:00.908
Three contributions.

00:01:00.988 --> 00:01:04.537
First,

00:01:04.617 --> 00:01:08.165
momentum SGD iterates converge almost surely to a
connected set of stationary points,

00:01:08.245 --> 00:01:11.793
even for non-convex losses.

00:01:11.873 --> 00:01:15.422
Second, it quantifies mSGD's rate, explaining
momentum's acceleration.

00:01:15.502 --> 00:01:19.050
Third, standard norm-form AdaGrad also converges
almost surely.

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:22.546
The analysis uses stochastic approximation,

00:01:22.626 --> 00:01:26.189
treating each algorithm as a noisy gradient flow.

00:01:26.269 --> 00:01:29.385
A stability lemma bounds the expected loss.

00:01:29.465 --> 00:01:31.987
Instead of uniformly bounded noise,

00:01:32.067 --> 00:01:39.272
they bound it relative to the loss. mSGD uses
decreasing Robbins-Monro steps with static momentum;

00:01:39.352 --> 00:01:42.320
AdaGrad's adaptive step needs new bounds.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:47.937
This work is entirely theoretical: no datasets,
benchmarks, or experiments,

00:01:48.017 --> 00:01:49.426
only proven theorems.

00:01:49.506 --> 00:01:54.530
The losses are non-negative and differentiable with
Lipschitz gradients,

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:59.280
but possibly non-convex, like sine-squared and
quartic polynomials.

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:03.680
The headline is two almost-sure convergence theorems.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:10.023
Theorem 1: momentum SGD iterates converge to a
connected set of stationary points.

00:02:10.103 --> 00:02:13.271
Theorem 3: standard AdaGrad does the same.

00:02:13.351 --> 00:02:17.990
Both strengthen earlier subsequence and
time-average results.

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:19.887
In place of experiments,

00:02:19.967 --> 00:02:25.142
the paper studies how the convergence rate depends
on the momentum coefficient.

00:02:25.222 --> 00:02:27.736
At zero it recovers SGD's rate exactly;

00:02:27.816 --> 00:02:33.790
toward one it shrinks the coefficient and pushes
the time-average rate to order one over T.

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:36.933
The key numbers are convergence rates.

00:02:37.013 --> 00:02:41.944
Momentum SGD's expected squared gradient norm
decays exponentially,

00:02:42.024 --> 00:02:47.929
with exponent scaling as summed step sizes over p
times one-minus-alpha squared.

00:02:48.009 --> 00:02:53.240
Alpha zero recovers the SGD rate; near one it
reaches order one over T.

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:55.694
Reassuringly for practitioners,

00:02:55.774 --> 00:03:01.877
under mild conditions both fixed-momentum SGD and
standard AdaGrad converge almost surely

00:03:01.957 --> 00:03:04.795
to stationary points of non-convex losses,

00:03:04.875 --> 00:03:10.700
and momentum provably accelerates SGD, narrowing
the gap between theory and practice.
